A||||5||sjp777||Removing front lower control arms||||5||
Z||000000||sjp777||04-07-2001||10:38 PM||||I'm trying to remove the front lower control arms on an 88 to replace the rubber bushings with poly. Problem is, I can't get the bolts out that hold it to the frame. The nuts come off no problem, but the bolts won't slide out and there is no room to get a good whack on them with a hammer. I thought about burning out the rubber with a torch 'cause I need to anyway, but I'm thinking I'll still end up with the sleeves stuck to the bolts. Any ideas?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Bob Englert||04-07-2001||10:43 PM||||Try soaking the bolts and sleeves with a lot of penetrating oil for a few days.  <P>The same thing happened to me with I rebuilt the front suspension on my first 85 SE.  On two of the bolts I had to saw the bolt out with a resipricating saw.  It was no fun at all.  PB Blaster and a torch didn't work for me.<P>Good luck...<P><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000002||sjp777||04-07-2001||10:47 PM||||I tried penetrating oil. It doesn't seem to penetrate very well.<P>Is nothing on these cars easy?!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Frank Sombers||04-07-2001||10:57 PM||||Dear SJP777:<BR>In answer to your question.......No! But, it<BR>does give you a feeling of accomplishment and<BR>creates a "bond" between you and your car when you complete a project.  <BR>Remember, it could be a lot worse...you could<BR>own a Jaguar!||||reg||1
Z||000004||DRH||04-08-2001||01:40 AM||||Go into the Ogre's Cave link at the bottom of most pages on here. He has a whole article on Bushings in the Chassis section. One suggestion in the article is to hit the seam in the sleeve with a punch to break it loose from the bolt.<P>I plan on starting this project tomorrow so I've been trying to read up on tips...||||reg||1
Z||000005||sjp777||04-08-2001||02:13 AM||||Let us know how it goes||||reg||1
